<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>The Dead Hang Workout</h2>
      <p>This dead hang workout is a single 30-minute session that combines dead hang sets with grip variation holds and complementary pulling exercises. Each block targets a different component of hand, forearm and shoulder performance.</p>
      <p>Start with this workout on 3 non-consecutive days a week and track your holds. Gains vary with your starting level. These doses are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
      <p>You need a pull-up bar and two pieces of optional equipment: dumbbells or kettlebells for farmer's walks and weight plates for pinch holds. A secure <a href="/equipment-guide/">pull-up bar</a> handles every hanging movement in this program.</p>

      <h2>Warm-Up (2 to 3 Minutes)</h2>
      <p>A warm-up helps you feel ready for the first hang; evidence that it prevents injury is limited. Spend two to three minutes on your wrists, shoulders and forearms before touching the bar.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Arm circles:</strong> 15 forward and 15 backward. Start small and increase the diameter with each rep.</li>
        <li><strong>Wrist circles:</strong> 10 slow circles in each direction per wrist. Keep the motion smooth and use the full range.</li>
        <li><strong>Band pull-aparts:</strong> 15 reps with a light resistance band. Hold the band at shoulder width with arms extended. Pull it apart until it touches your chest.</li>
        <li><strong>Short hang:</strong> one or two hangs of 10 to 15 seconds at easy effort. Grip the bar and hang with relaxed shoulders. Shake out your hands after release. This also confirms your equipment is secure.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A short warm-up is enough to start. The <a href="/mobility/">mobility guide</a> has the full warm-up routine.</p>

      <h2>Workout Block 1: Dead Hang Sets</h2>
      <p>This block is the core of the dead hang workout. Four sets of controlled holds build grip endurance and shoulder tolerance under sustained load.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Block 1 Protocol</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sets:</strong> 4</li>
          <li><strong>Duration:</strong> Hold until just before your grip gives out</li>
          <li><strong>Rest:</strong> 90 seconds between sets</li>
          <li><strong>Grip:</strong> Overhand (pronated) at shoulder width</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p>Grab the bar with a pronated grip at shoulder width. Step off the platform and let your full bodyweight hang. Keep your arms straight and your core braced lightly. Breathe through your nose in a slow rhythm.</p>
      <p>Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out and step down under control. Do not hang to failure or drop. Grip fatigue feels like a burning sensation in the forearms. Sharp joint pain means stop immediately.</p>
      <p>Record your time for each set. Hold times usually fall across sets. An example pattern is 25, 20, 17 and 14 seconds (an illustration, not data). Track these numbers weekly to see your progress.</p>
      <p>Rest 90 seconds between sets. Walk around and shake out your hands during rest, and save stretching for the cool-down.</p>

      <h2>Workout Block 2: Grip Variations</h2>
      <p>Block 1 trains your standard overhand grip. Block 2 targets different forearm muscles by changing hand position. Each variation loads the wrist and fingers at a different angle.</p>

      <h3>Underhand Hang (Supinated Grip)</h3>
      <p>Grip the bar with palms facing you at shoulder width. Hang for 20 seconds. An underhand grip uses the biceps more than an overhand grip and puts the biceps tendon under stretch. Stop if the front of the elbow hurts. Perform 2 sets with 60 seconds rest.</p>

      <h3>Neutral Grip Hang</h3>
      <p>Use parallel grip handles if your bar has them. Hang for 20 seconds with palms facing each other. Perform 2 sets with 60 seconds rest.</p>

      <h3>Active Hang</h3>
      <p>Grip the bar overhand and pull your shoulder blades down and back without bending your elbows. Hold this <a href="/deadhang-variations/">active hang position</a> for 20 seconds. Active hangs practice the shoulder blade position used at the start of a pull-up. Perform 2 sets with 60 seconds rest.</p>
      <p>Keep each variation hold at 20 seconds even if you can go longer. The goal here is exposure to different grip angles rather than maximal effort. Save your hardest holds for Block 1.</p>

      <h2>Workout Block 3: Complementary Exercises</h2>
      <p>Dead hangs train support grip. This block adds pinch grip, loaded carries and forearm isolation work for variety in your total grip training.</p>

      <h3>Farmer's Walks</h3>
      <p>Pick up a heavy dumbbell or kettlebell in each hand. Stand tall with shoulders packed and core tight. Walk for 30 seconds at a controlled pace. Perform 3 sets with 60 seconds rest between sets.</p>
      <p>Choose a weight that makes your grip burn by the 25-second mark. Farmer's walks train support grip, core stability and loaded carries in one movement. Start with a weight you can carry for 30 seconds with good posture.</p>

      <h3>Plate Pinches</h3>
      <p>Place two weight plates smooth-side-out and pinch them together with one hand. Hold for 15 seconds per hand. Perform 3 sets with 45 seconds rest. Plate pinches train the thumb and pinch grip. Dead hangs load these less.</p>
      <p>Start with two 5 kg (about 11 lb) plates per hand. Increase to 7.5 kg plates once you can hold 15 seconds without slipping. Read more about plate pinch technique in the <a href="/complementary-exercises/">complementary exercises guide</a>.</p>

      <h3>Wrist Curls</h3>
      <p>Sit on a bench with your forearms resting on your thighs and wrists hanging over the edge. Curl a light barbell or dumbbells up through full wrist flexion. Perform 2 sets of 15 reps. Follow with 2 sets of 15 reverse wrist curls (palms facing down).</p>
      <p>Use a weight you can control through the full range. Wrist curls isolate the forearm flexors and extensors that support your dead hang grip. Keep the reps high and the tempo slow.</p>

      <h2>Cool-Down (5 Minutes)</h2>
      <p>Spend five minutes stretching the forearms and shoulders you just loaded.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Forearm flexor stretch:</strong> Extend your arm with the palm up. Use your other hand to pull your fingers back toward your body. Hold 30 seconds per arm.</li>
        <li><strong>Forearm extensor stretch:</strong> Extend your arm with the palm down. Use your other hand to press the back of your hand toward your body. Hold 30 seconds per arm.</li>
        <li><strong>Shoulder stretch:</strong> Reach one arm across your chest and pull it closer with the opposite hand. Hold 30 seconds per side.</li>
        <li><strong>Child's pose:</strong> Kneel on the floor and sit back on your heels. Extend your arms forward on the ground and rest your forehead down. Hold 60 seconds.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Weekly Programming</h2>
      <p>Start with this dead hang workout on 3 non-consecutive days a week. Add a fourth day only if you stay pain free. Leave at least one rest day between hard sessions.</p>

      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Day</th>
            <th scope="col">Session Type</th>
            <th scope="col">Focus</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Monday</td>
            <td>Heavy</td>
            <td>Full workout (Blocks 1-3)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Tuesday</td>
            <td>Rest</td>
            <td>Active recovery or light stretching</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Wednesday</td>
            <td>Light</td>
            <td>Block 1 only (4 sets, best controlled hold)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Thursday</td>
            <td>Rest</td>
            <td>Active recovery</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Friday</td>
            <td>Heavy</td>
            <td>Full workout (Blocks 1-3)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Saturday</td>
            <td>Optional</td>
            <td>Rest, or two easy hangs only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Sunday</td>
            <td>Rest</td>
            <td>Full rest day</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <p>Heavy days include all three blocks plus warm-up and cool-down. Light days focus on hang sets only with reduced volume. The rest days between the harder sessions keep the weekly load manageable. The <a href="/deadhang-frequency/">dead hang frequency guide</a> explains the rule.</p>
      <p>A lighter week about every fourth week is a common coaching convention, not a tested rule. In that week cut your total hang time by about half and drop the complementary exercises. Resume full volume the week after.</p>

      <h2>Scaling the Workout</h2>
      <h3>Beginner Modifications</h3>
      <p>If you cannot hold the bar for 10 seconds yet use a loop band or keep your feet on a box or the ground. Foot support or the band takes part of your weight. Record the assist you used so tests stay comparable. Perform the same 4-set structure with assisted hangs. Move to full bodyweight hangs once you reach 15-second holds.</p>
      <p>Reduce Block 2 to one set per variation instead of two. Drop plate pinches from Block 3 until your base grip handles 20-second hangs consistently. Follow the <a href="/beginner-deadhang/">beginner dead hang guide</a> for step-by-step progression.</p>

      <h3>Advanced Additions</h3>
      <p>Add weight once you hold a comfortable, pain-free bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds on all four Block 1 sets. Strap on a dip belt and start with 5 to 10 lbs (2 to 5 kg). Holds get shorter with added load. Add about 5 lbs once every set is clean and stop adding weight if your elbows or fingers hurt.</p>
      <p>Once you meet the one-arm prerequisite of a controlled two-arm hang of about 60 seconds you can replace the active hang in Block 2 with a one-arm assisted hang. Grip the bar with one hand and hold the wrist with the other for support. This is a first step toward single-arm work.</p>
      <p>Add towel hangs as a fifth Block 2 variation. Drape a towel over the bar and grip both ends. A towel thickens the grip surface and makes holds harder. Hold for 15 to 20 seconds per set.</p>

